The difference between AC -42 and AC -48 is actually quite large.  At -42,
Orcus will whittle away your HP in about 50 turns.  At AC -48, you can
just stand there and take a beating and wait for him to summon the big D
as long as you'd like.  (My recent S ascension, which I have not yet
posted, involved enchanting up a small shield for the sole purpose of
baiting.)

Assume we start with a combat wombat with a shield: AC -47, +7 Frosty
Now, take away the shield and let him #twoweapon: AC -40, +7 Frosty and +7
SS
Instead, make him a wizard, give him +1 enchantment on 7 pieces of armor,
but make him use only a small shield: AC -53, +7 Frosty

This makes the single-weapon wizard significantly better of a wombat than
the single-weapon wombat, and probably about as good as the twoweaponer
(my AC -42 S was quite a bit more dangerous, but much more fragile, than
the AC -54 characters I've walked around with.)  So from a game balance
perspective, giving casters a higher max enchantment probably isn't a good
idea -- late-game W are already absurdly powerful.

Perhaps a suitable modification would be to make various casters more
likely to get good enchantment off of scrolls (say, W get +2 70% of the
time, while C only get it 40%, and everyone else is in between.)  Perhaps
also slightly modify the chances of vaporizing armor, so that casters are
a little more likely to be able to overenchant.  This would give casters a
bit of an enchantment bonus early (when that +2 vs +1 is going to make a
huge difference) and save them a few scrolls late, but not give anyone a
very big enchantment bonus for the endgame.  More casters might actually
create a piece or two of +6 armor, but you won't see (m)any ascending in
*all* +6 armor, like you would if they just had higher max enchantment
ability.



